hadiths[9]{hadithnumber,text}:
"24420","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I am amazed at the decree of Allah for the believer. The affair of the believer is all good, and that is only for the believer: if prosperity befalls him and he gives thanks, it is good for him, and if adversity befalls him and he is patient, it is good for him."
"24421","Yazid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When the people of Paradise enter Paradise, they will be called out: O people of Paradise, you have an appointment with Allah that you have not yet seen. They will say: What is it? Has He not whitened our faces, moved us away from the Fire, and admitted us into Paradise? He said: Then the veil will be removed. He said: They will look at Him, and by Allah, Allah will not have given them anything more beloved to them than it. Then he recited: For those who do good is the best reward and more. And he said once: When the people of Paradise enter."
"24422","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Zuhayr, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Hamzah ibn Suhayb, that Suhayb used to be called Abu Yahya, and he said that he was from the Arabs, and he used to feed food in abundance. Umar said to him: O Suhayb, why are you called Abu Yahya when you have no child, and you say you are from the Arabs, and you feed food in abundance, and that is extravagance with wealth? Suhayb said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave me the kunyah Abu Yahya. As for your remark about lineage, I am a man from al-Namir ibn Qasit, from the people of al-Mawsil, but I was taken captive as a small boy, heedless of my family and my people. As for your remark about food, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say: The best of you are those who feed food and return the greeting of peace. That is what carries me to feed food."
"24423","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed, he would whisper something I did not understand, and he would not inform us of it. He said: Did you notice that about me? We said: Yes. He said: I remembered a prophet among the prophets who was given troops from his people. He said: Who can match these, or who can stand up to these, or other such words. Then it was revealed to him: Choose for your people one of three: either that I set against them an enemy from other than them, or hunger, or death. So he consulted his people about that, and they said: You are the prophet of Allah; all of that is up to you, choose for us. So he stood for prayer, and when they were frightened, they would hasten to prayer, and he prayed as Allah willed. He said: Then he said: O Lord, as for an enemy from other than them, no, and hunger, no, but death. So death was set upon them, and seventy thousand of them died. My whispering that you see is that I say: O Allah, by You I fight, and by You I assail, and there is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"24424","Rawh narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say when he met the enemy: O Allah, by You I move, by You I assail, and by You I fight."
"24425","Zakariyya ibn Adi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Hamzah ibn Suhayb, from his father, who said: He said to Umar: As for your remark that I took a kunyah though I have no child, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave me the kunyah Abu Yahya. As for your remark about extravagance with food, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The best of you are those who feed food, or: those who feed food."
"24426","Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, who said: While the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was sitting with his companions, he laughed. He said: Will you not ask me what I am laughing at? They said: O Messenger of Allah, what are you laughing at? He said: I am amazed at the affair of the believer: his affair is all good. If what he loves befalls him, he praises Allah, and it is good for him; and if what he dislikes befalls him and he is patient, it is good for him. And no one's affair is all good for him except the believer."
"24427","My father said, and Affan narrated it to us as well: Sulayman narrated it to us, Thabit narrated to us, this very wording, and I think he erred. This is the wording of Hammad. He narrated to us, he said: Sulayman narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, something like the wording of Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman, and that is from his book; he read it to us."
"24428","Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Thabit informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Suhayb, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There was a king among those who were before you, and he had a magician. When the magician grew old, he said to the king: I have grown old in age, and my term has come; hand over to me a boy so that I may teach him magic. So he handed over to him a boy, and he used to teach him magic. Between the magician and the king there was a monk. The boy came upon the monk and heard some of his speech, and his manner and speech pleased him. When he came to the magician, he would beat him and say: What has detained you? And when he came to his family, they would beat him and say: What has detained you? So he complained of that to the monk, who said: When the magician wants to beat you, say: My family detained me; and when your family wants to beat you, say: The magician detained me. He said: While he was like that, one day he came upon a dreadful, huge beast that had blocked the people, so they could not pass. He said: Today I shall know whether the affair of the monk is more beloved to Allah or the affair of the magician. So he took a stone and said: O Allah, if the affair of the monk is more beloved to You and more pleasing to You than the magician, then kill this beast so that the people may pass. He threw it and killed it, and the people passed. He informed the monk of that, and he said: O my son, you are better than me, and you will be tested; if you are tested, do not point to me. The boy used to cure the blind and other diseases and heal them. A companion of the king had become blind; he heard of him and came to him with many gifts and said: Cure me, and all that is here is yours. He said: I do not cure anyone; only Allah, Mighty and Majestic, cures. If you believe in Him, I will supplicate Allah for you and He will cure you. So he believed, and he supplicated Allah for him and He cured him. Then he came to the king and sat with him as he used to sit. The king said to him: O so-and-so, who restored your sight to you? He said: My Lord. He said: I? He said: No, but my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He said: Do you have a lord other than me? He said: Yes. He kept torturing him until he pointed him to the boy. He sent for him and said: O my son, your magic has reached the point that you cure the blind and the leper and these diseases. He said: I do not cure anyone; none cures but Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He said: I? He said: No. He said: Do you have a lord other than me? He said: Yes, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. He also seized him with torture and kept at him until he pointed to the monk. The monk was brought and told: Return from your religion, but he refused. So he placed the saw in the parting of his head until its two halves fell. He said to the blind man: Return from your religion, but he refused, so he placed the saw in the parting of his head until its two halves fell to the ground. He said to the boy: Return from your religion, but he refused. So he sent him with a group to such-and-such mountain and said: When you reach its peak, if he returns from his religion, otherwise throw him down from above it. They took him, and when they ascended the mountain with him, he said: O Allah, suffice me against them however You will. The mountain shook with them and they were all thrown down, and the boy came walking until he entered upon the king. He said: What happened to your companions? He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sufficed me against them. So he sent him with a group in a small boat and said: When you venture with him into the sea, if he returns from his religion, otherwise drown him. They ventured with him into the sea, and the boy said: O Allah, suffice me against them however You will. They all drowned, and the boy came walking until he entered upon the king. He said: What happened to your companions? He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sufficed me against them. Then he said to the king: You will not kill me until you do what I command you. If you do what I command you, you will kill me; otherwise you will not be able to kill me. He said: What is it? He said: Gather the people in an open plain, then crucify me on a trunk, take an arrow from my quiver, then say: In the name of Allah, the Lord of the boy. If you do that, you will kill me. So he did so, placed the arrow in the belly of the bow, then shot, saying: In the name of Allah, the Lord of the boy. He placed the arrow in his temple, and the boy placed his hand on the spot of the arrow and died. The people said: We believe in the Lord of the boy. It was said to the king: Do you see what you feared? By Allah, it has befallen you: all the people have believed. So he ordered the mouths of the roads, and ditches were dug in them, fires were kindled in them, and he said: Whoever returns from his religion, leave him; otherwise cast him into it. He said: They would rush into it and push one another. A woman came with a son of hers whom she was nursing, and she seemed to hesitate to fall into the fire. The boy said: O mother, be patient, for you are upon the truth."
